# Environment variables for the Netlify Functions in netlify/functions/.
#
# Copy to .env for local development (`netlify dev`), and set the same eleven
# values in Netlify under Site configuration -> Environment variables.
# (Ten until the second course: STRIPE_COURSE_PRICE_ID became one variable per
# course, each holding a comma-separated list.)
#
# URL is the twelfth name the functions read, and the one you do NOT set: see
# the note at the bottom.
#
# .env is gitignored. Never commit real values — this repository is PUBLIC.
#
# Ten of the eleven are required. A missing variable does not fail the build; the
# affected function fails silently at runtime, so the site looks fine while
# signups, logins, purchases or the directory quietly stop working.
# GA4_API_SECRET is the exception: without it everything still works and only
# revenue reporting goes missing, loudly, in the function log.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Supabase — database and auth
# Used by: auth-login, auth-register, auth-verify, auth-forgot-password,
#          newsletter-subscribe, stripe-webhook
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Project URL. Public by design; also hardcoded in course/config.js.
SUPABASE_URL=https://xxxxxxxxxxxx.supabase.co

# Anon/public key. Safe to expose — it is already in course/config.js and
# relies on row-level security being ENABLED on every table.
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# Service-role key. SECRET. Bypasses row-level security completely.
# Dashboard: Project Settings -> API Keys -> service_role
SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=eyJhbGciOi...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Stripe — course payments
# Used by: stripe-webhook
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Secret API key. Dashboard: Developers -> API keys
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=sk_live_...

# Signing secret for the webhook endpoint. Tied to one specific endpoint URL,
# so it MUST be regenerated whenever the site URL changes. Create the endpoint
# at <site>/.netlify/functions/stripe-webhook subscribed to
# these three events, then copy that endpoint's secret here:
#   checkout.session.completed
#   checkout.session.async_payment_succeeded
#   checkout.session.async_payment_failed
#
# All three matter. A card payment is already paid when the session completes,
# but an asynchronous method (a bank debit, Klarna) completes the session
# UNPAID and settles later. stripe-webhook.js refuses to grant access on an
# unpaid session, so without async_payment_succeeded subscribed such a
# customer would be charged and never let in.
# Verified at netlify/functions/stripe-webhook.js:21-24.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=whsec_...

# Stripe PRICE IDs per course — NOT payment links.
#
# OPTIONAL NOW. The Catering Profit System's two live prices are registered in
# netlify/functions/lib/courses.js, so the webhook works with none of these
# set. A price id is an identifier rather than a credential — it is what
# client-side Stripe integrations pass around — and this repository already
# hardcodes the payment links those prices sit behind, which are strictly more
# revealing. Making a purchase depend on an unset variable bought nothing and
# cost a founding buyer their access.
#
# Set these to ADD a price without a deploy. They are unioned with the
# registry, so a variable can only ever add a price, never drop a working one.
#
# stripe-webhook.js matches these against the checkout session's line items to
# decide which course a payment grants. If one is wrong or missing, customers
# are charged and receive nothing.
#
# COMMA-SEPARATED, because a course can have several live prices at once. The
# Catering Profit System sells at $197 evergreen and $147 to the first ten
# founding members: separate payment links, so separate Price objects, and
# BOTH must be listed. Listing only one means every buyer at the other price
# pays and is told their payment "is not for the course".
#
# To find them: Dashboard -> Products -> the course -> each pricing row ->
# API ID. A price id starts `price_`; a payment link starts `plink_` or is a
# buy.stripe.com URL, and neither will ever match.
#
# A price id must appear under exactly ONE course. The same id under two
# courses makes every purchase ambiguous, and the webhook then refuses to
# guess: it grants nothing and emails you instead.
#
# Leaving BOTH unset skips the check entirely and treats every completed
# checkout as the Catering Profit System — including the $350 Snapshot. The
# function logs a warning saying so on every payment.
STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_PROFIT=price_...,price_...
STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_PEOPLE=price_...

# Price IDs for The Complete Catering System: Profit + People — the bundle
# that grants BOTH courses at once.
# Comma-separated like the others, so a founding or launch bundle price can
# sit alongside the standard one.
#
# A bundle works whichever shape it takes in Stripe. One Price for a single
# "both courses" product belongs here. A payment link holding one line item
# per course needs nothing here at all: the webhook grants the union of
# everything a session matches, so both courses are granted either way.
#
# Leaving this unset while selling a single-Price bundle means the bundle
# matches nothing and the buyer gets no course.
STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_BUNDLE=price_...

# Price IDs for the UPGRADE — someone who already owns one course paying the
# difference for the other. $100, which is $297 - $197 and also $247 - $147,
# so it is the same figure at the standard and the founding prices.
#
# This price grants BOTH courses, because one Stripe price cannot know which
# one the buyer is missing; the course they already hold is a no-op on the way
# in, so it works from either direction.
#
# Which also makes it a $100 link worth $297 to anyone who finds it. Stripe
# payment links cannot be gated on a previous purchase and an unlisted link is
# still a shareable URL, so the webhook checks the buyer already holds one of
# the two courses. If they hold neither it grants NOTHING and emails you:
# they have underpaid, and refunding versus billing the difference is not a
# decision to make automatically.
STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_UPGRADE=price_...

# Superseded by the two above, but STILL READ, and deliberately so.
#
# A single price id was all there was before there were two courses. The
# registry adds whatever is here to the Catering Profit System's list, on top
# of STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_PROFIT rather than instead of it.
#
# That is what makes a deploy safe before the new variables are set: without
# it the gate would match nothing, and the webhook's no-prices-configured
# fallback would then treat every completed checkout as a course purchase —
# handing the course to $350 Snapshot buyers, whom this one price id currently
# excludes correctly.
#
# Safe to delete from Netlify once STRIPE_PRICE_IDS_CATERING_PROFIT lists
# every live price for the course, this one included.
STRIPE_COURSE_PRICE_ID=price_...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Google Analytics 4 — server-side revenue reporting
# Used by: stripe-webhook
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Measurement Protocol API secret. SECRET, and not the same thing as the
# measurement ID (G-778929FT8G), which is public and hardcoded in analytics.js
# and in stripe-webhook.js.
#
# Create it in GA4: Admin -> Data streams -> the web stream -> Measurement
# Protocol API secrets -> Create. The value is shown in full after creation.
#
# This is what lets stripe-webhook.js report what a purchase was actually
# worth. The browser cannot: it never learns the amount, and the $350 Snapshot
# finishes on Stripe's own booking page without ever returning to the site, so
# it is invisible from the front end entirely.
#
# Leave it unset and nothing breaks — no purchase event is sent at all, and
# the function logs a warning naming the session it could not report. The
# `purchase` conversion in GA4 will simply stay at zero.
GA4_API_SECRET=

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Resend — transactional email
# Used by: auth-register, auth-forgot-password, stripe-webhook
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# API key. Shown once on creation — if lost, create a new one.
# The code sends from noreply@trecoleman.com, so that domain must be verified
# in Resend with live DKIM/SPF records or delivery fails despite a valid key.
RESEND_API_KEY=re_...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Airtable — Virginia Neighbors directory
# Used by: airtable-proxy
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Personal access token. Shown once on creation.
# Needs read AND write scope on base appk7mBGffiWhowPC (table "Listings"),
# which is hardcoded at netlify/functions/airtable-proxy.js:3-4.
AIRTABLE_TOKEN=pat...

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Chat gate
# Used by: chat-auth
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Shared password for the premium AI chat. Self-chosen — nothing validates it
# against a previous value, so pick any strong string.
CHAT_PASSWORD=choose-a-strong-value

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# URL — set by Netlify, not by you
# Used by: auth-register, auth-forgot-password, stripe-webhook
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Netlify injects this at build and runtime as the site's primary address: the
# custom domain once one is attached, and the *.netlify.app address until then.
# The three functions above use it for links in outbound email.
#
# They used to hardcode https://trecoleman.com. While the custom domain was not
# attached that host failed TLS, so a customer could buy the course and get a
# confirmation email whose "log in" button went nowhere, and a password reset
# would point at a dead redirect target. Reading URL means the links follow
# wherever the site actually is, and start pointing at the custom domain by
# themselves the moment it is attached.
#
# Leave it unset locally and the code falls back to https://trecoleman.com.
# Do not add it in Netlify — setting it by hand overrides the real value.
# URL=https://example.netlify.app

# The from: addresses stay on trecoleman.com regardless. That is where Resend's
# DKIM record lives, and it is verified independently of where the site is
# served — confirmed present while the custom domain was still detached.
